963 0 obj
<<
/Title (The impact of this mental exhaustion extends far beyond the individual, severely degrading the quality of interpersonal communication and social cohesion. When a person is saturated with global-scale anxieties, they often experience "compassion fatigue," a state where the emotional bandwidth for empathy toward friends and family is depleted by the sheer volume of distant tragedies. This often manifests as "phubbing" or irritability in physical spaces, as the individual becomes more tethered to the urgent, digital simulation of crisis than to the nuanced, quiet reality of the people sitting directly across from them.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 965 0 R
/Next 960 0 R
>>
endobj
964 0 obj
<<
/Title (From The Editor)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 962 0 R
/Next 966 0 R
>>
endobj
965 0 obj
<<
/Title (Consequently, this neurological taxation exhausts our cognitive resources, making it increasingly difficult to engage in the high-level emotional regulation required for healthy daily functioning)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 967 0 R
/Next 963 0 R
>>
endobj
966 0 obj
<<
/Title (Alize G.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 964 0 R
/Next 968 0 R
>>
endobj
967 0 obj
<<
/Title (This evolutionary trap leads directly into a physiological state of chronic hyper-vigilance, as the constant stream of crisis imagery triggers a persistent release of cortisol and adrenaline. Unlike a physical threat that eventually passes, the digital world offers no resolution, keeping the amygdala in a state of permanent alarm.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 969 0 R
/Next 965 0 R
>>
endobj
